                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[b][i][Review copy was supplied by the developer for the purpose of review.][/i][/b]\n\nDespite not being the intended audience for these casual, physics-based puzzle games, Slash/Dots was quite surprising by how good of a game it is. Its simplicity of a concept doesn’t do the game its due diligence to the craftmanship behind how many small details were put into the design of its gameplay. Whether you prefer playing the game on your computer (as I have played 50/103 levels for an hour and a half) or on your mobile device like I have after work, Slash/Dots is a great little time-waster when you want something mentally stimulating enough to be a puzzle without being taxing enough on the brain.\n\n[h1]Sharpness is the Consistency to See Through New Challenges[/h1]\n\nAt its core, Slash/Dots is very approachable for all ages. The simple goal is to get two colored marbles to come into contact by drawing lines to cut away wood pieces to make one marble or the other (or both) propel forward. Sometimes these levels will tax you beyond simply chipping away the level such as using various mechanisms like wheels or rotating arms; other levels may utilize the slowdown feature when drawing to time your cuts more precisely. These latter puzzles may not always feel the most rewarding to solve as the timing/physics can sometimes feel random; however, most puzzles often are focused around its other mechanics, the physics as a system and the new obstacles.\n\nPerhaps the toughest obstacle when making a good physics-based puzzle game is nailing down the right amount of consistency as well as giving players leniency to solve problems. Slash/Dots manages to balance these two goals exceptionally well as I replayed every puzzle twice to make sure none of the solutions were flukes. If I found a process to solve it such as making specific shapes or a level of thickness/steepness, then it would usually result in a workable solution. Instead of making the marbles work a specific way or requiring them to be together when they stop, the game feels a lot more forgiving as you only need to maintain their inertia to get them into contact. This type of challenge makes for an addictive quality more so from the process of solving it than simply having the answer. \n\nWhat makes these levels so enjoyable is their piece-by-piece nature that adds more and more complexity and new ideas to solve problems. Difficulty has a nice smoothness throughout levels, and if you failed to understand how to solve a given level you either need to learn something new or you need to build on what you learned previously. Sometimes the best method is to be playful with everything, seeing what parts will move or contort based on your actions, then making a solution from your observations. This is the kind of thrill I got from playing The Witness because Slash/Dots isn’t about solving (arbitrary) problems but learning how to use systems to make solutions. \n\n [h1]”Please, Devs, May We Have More?”[/h1]\n\nWhile a level-editor would only make the game more enjoyable as well as foster a PC community, if the puzzles alone can excite you there will be plenty to sate an inquisitive mind. At roughly 3 – 4 hours, Slash/Dots offers enough to warrant its price-tag that its limited content is a minor concern. It’s simply good enough to leave you wanting more, and that is a good feeling to come away with when a novel concept doesn’t feel worn out by the end.",
